# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: ci-native-child-pipeline-creation-fails — native:pipeline cannot create its child, so every pipeline is red with zero failed jobs
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -

* Symptom

Since about 16:49 UTC on 2026-08-19, every pipeline — merge requests AND
master — finishes RED while *no job has failed*:

: 15 success, 0 failed, 15 skipped, 1 manual

The red comes from the =native:pipeline= bridge alone:

: bridge: failed   failure_reason: downstream_pipeline_creation_failed
: child pipeline:  failed, 0 jobs, created_at == updated_at

The child is created and dies in the same second, with no jobs at all.

This matters beyond cosmetics: a red master makes every later merge
request look red too, and =glab mr merge= REFUSES to merge on a failed
pipeline (the project itself allows it —
=only_allow_merge_if_pipeline_succeeds= is false — so the API still
works, but the normal path is blocked).

* What has been ruled out

Checked before concluding, because the obvious suspect was the branch
that first showed it (MR !107):

- *not the branch's content.* MR !107 changes six files: two =script=
  blocks in =documentation= / =release:documentation=, a Makefile
  variable, a version stamp, and two documentation files. Nothing that
  can affect child-pipeline creation.
- *not the child config.* =.gitlab/native.yml= is byte-identical to
  master, and =POST /projects/:id/ci/lint= on its content answers
  =valid: true=, no errors, no warnings.
- *not the parent config.* =glab ci lint= passes.
- *not runner availability.* =detect:runners= reported
  =MACOS_RUNNER=online= and =WINDOWS_RUNNER=online= in the very pipeline
  whose child failed. The child's gate rules end in =when: manual=
  anyway, so an offline runner yields manual jobs, never zero jobs.
- *not transient.* Retrying the bridge produced another empty child;
  re-creating the whole pipeline from a fresh commit produced a third.
- *not caused by the merge.* Master pipeline 2773471716 (54b44c6, before
  the window) SUCCEEDED with the same configuration; 2773597223
  (90fd7ed, after) fails the same way. Nothing between them touches the
  native pipeline.
- *not a runner/quota starvation of the obvious kind.* No pipelines were
  running at the time of the check, and shared runners are disabled
  (self-hosted only), so CI-minutes quota does not apply.

* The cause — a quota, and we fill it ourselves

pjb read the message off the bridge job page (it is not exposed by the
API; =GET /projects/:id/jobs/<bridge-id>/trace= answers 404 for a
bridge):

: Project exceeded the allowed number of jobs in active pipelines. Retry later.

So it is a LIMIT, not a fault — and what fills it is our own design.

*Child pipelines here never complete, on purpose.* The parent says so:

#+begin_quote
NB: no =strategy: depend= — when the runner is offline the child's jobs
are =manual= and the child pipeline never "completes", so depending on
it would hang this parent job.
#+end_quote

That comment anticipated the child never completing. What it did not
anticipate is that a child which never completes stays *ACTIVE* for
ever, and its jobs keep counting against the project's
jobs-in-active-pipelines cap. Every push adds one more immortal child.

Measured when the limit bit: *17 child pipelines in `running`*, the
oldest created at 10:08 that morning, still "running" at 17:50 — one per
pipeline of the day, none of which could ever finish because their jobs
are =manual= and nobody plays them.

The reason it surfaced TODAY is simply volume: four merge requests, each
re-pipelined several times through rebases. The design has been leaking
one immortal pipeline per push all along; today it crossed the cap.

Note this also explains why the earlier evidence looked contradictory:
the same configuration succeeded before ~16:49 and failed after, on both
master and the merge requests, with nothing in between touching the
native pipeline. It was never about the content — it was a counter
filling up.

* Unblocked (2026-08-19 ~17:50)

15 stale child pipelines cancelled — every never-completing one except
the child of the merge request being tested at that moment. The bridge
was then retried on master's pipeline: the child was created, the bridge
went green, and *master's pipeline 2773597223 is now =success=*.

That is a broom, not a fix. The next few days of pushes will fill the
cap again.

* The actual fix, to decide

The child's gate ends in =when: manual, allow_failure: true= — the
"clickable, non-blocking fallback" for an offline runner. That fallback
is what never terminates. Candidates:

1. *Auto-cancel redundant pipelines* + =interruptible: true= on the
   child's jobs. A new push then cancels the superseded child for that
   ref automatically. Smallest change, keeps the fallback, and kills the
   accumulation at its source for every ref that gets pushed again.
   Does nothing for a ref pushed once and left.
2. *A scheduled sweep*: a cron job cancelling child pipelines still
   active after N hours. Reliable, but it is the broom above, automated
   — it treats the symptom for ever.
3. *Drop the manual fallback when the runner is offline* (=when: never=).
   The child then completes... except when EVERY job is skipped, which
   makes an empty child, which is the same red we started from. So this
   one needs a keep-alive job to be viable.
4. *Do not use a child pipeline for the offline case at all* — the
   heaviest change, and the only one that removes the mechanism rather
   than managing it.

Option 1 is the recommendation: it is a project setting plus one
keyword, and it makes the common case (a branch pushed repeatedly)
self-cleaning. Option 2 alongside it would cover the abandoned refs that
option 1 misses.

* DONE — option 1 (2026-08-19, clautolisp 1.8.56)

pjb: « Implémente l'option 1. »

*The project setting was already =enabled=.* =auto_cancel_pending_pipelines=
has been on all along — including through the whole day the 17 children
accumulated. So the setting was never the missing half, and saying "turn
on auto-cancel" would have changed nothing.

What was missing is the other half, and it explains why the setting was
inert: *GitLab auto-cancels a superseded pipeline only when ALL its
pending/running jobs are interruptible*, and a single job without the
keyword pins the whole pipeline. Three of the child's twenty-six jobs
declared =interruptible: true= individually — which is the exact shape of
half-measure that achieves nothing, because the other twenty-three held
the pipeline open regardless.

=.gitlab/native.yml= now declares it once, for every job:

: default:
:   interruptible: true

Both configs re-validated through =POST /ci/lint= (the child as a
standalone config) and =glab ci lint= (the parent).

On a release tag this changes nothing in practice: auto-cancel fires only
when a NEWER pipeline supersedes one on the same ref, and a release tag
is not re-pushed.

** What this does not cover, and how it will be known

Auto-cancel needs a SUPERSEDING pipeline. A ref pushed once and then
abandoned — a branch whose merge request is merged from the last push,
say — leaves its child with nothing to supersede it, and that child still
lives for ever. That is the gap option 2 (a scheduled sweep) exists for,
and it is not implemented here.

And the mechanism itself is only verifiable by watching: the next few
pushes to a branch should leave exactly ONE active child per ref instead
of one per push. Worth checking with

: GET /projects/:id/pipelines?source=parent_pipeline

— which is the query that matters, and the one that misled the first
diagnosis: the plain pipelines listing EXCLUDES child pipelines, so
"0 running pipelines" was reported while seventeen children were running.


* DONE — option 2, le balayage nocturne (2026-08-20, clautolisp 1.8.58)

pjb : « Ça me semble bien, de faire un option 2 en nightly. Vers 03:00Z »

C'est la moitié que l'option 1 laissait ouverte, et elle est nommée plus
haut : l'auto-annulation exige une pipeline QUI EN REMPLACE une autre,
donc une ref poussée une fois puis abandonnée — typiquement la branche
d'une MR fusionnée depuis sa dernière poussée — laisse un enfant que
rien ne viendra jamais remplacer. C'est précisément la population qui
avait rempli le quota.

- =scripts/cancel-stale-child-pipelines.py= annule les pipelines enfants
  encore actives au-delà de =SWEEP_MAX_AGE_HOURS= (12 par défaut) ;
- le job =sweep:stale-child-pipelines= le lance, et il ne tourne QUE sur
  une pipeline planifiée ;
- l'inverse est vrai aussi, et c'est l'erreur qu'il fallait éviter : les
  13 jobs et 3 templates qui n'ont rien à faire dans un balayage portent
  désormais l'ancre =.not-on-schedule=, pour qu'une pipeline nocturne ne
  rejoue pas la CI complète chaque nuit. =make check-nightly-sweep-isolation=
  vérifie cette séparation dans les deux sens, donc elle ne peut pas se
  défaire en silence.

Deux pièges rencontrés en l'écrivant, consignés parce qu'ils ne se
devinent pas :

- la requête qui compte est =pipelines?source=parent_pipeline= — le
  listing ordinaire EXCLUT les pipelines enfants, ce qui avait déjà
  égaré le premier diagnostic (« 0 running » pendant que dix-sept
  enfants tournaient) ;
- le script échoue bruyamment si une annulation est refusée, plutôt que
  de compter un succès : un balai qui balaie zéro pipeline en silence
  serait exactement le mode de panne que ce ticket décrit.

** Planification activée le 2026-08-20

Le schedule 4397979 (=0 3 * * *=, UTC, ref master) existait mais était
INACTIF tant que la MR n'était pas fusionnée — un schedule actif pointant
sur un job inexistant n'aurait rien balayé. Il est activé depuis que
!108 est dans master ; premier déclenchement le 2026-08-21 à 03:00Z.

** Ce qui reste à observer avant de considérer le sujet clos

Rien à écrire, seulement à regarder — et c'est pour cela que le STATUS
reste TODO :

1. après le passage du 2026-08-21 03:00Z, vérifier que le job a tourné,
   qu'il a annulé les enfants abandonnés et lui seul (aucun job de CI
   ordinaire dans cette pipeline planifiée) ;
2. sur quelques jours, vérifier via =pipelines?source=parent_pipeline=
   qu'il reste UNE seule pipeline enfant active par ref au lieu d'une
   par poussée.

Les deux ensemble ferment le ticket. Tant qu'ils ne sont pas constatés,
la mécanique est écrite et fusionnée, pas prouvée.
* Affected merge requests

MR !107 was merged through the API rather than =glab= for this reason,
with every one of its 15 real jobs green. That is recorded here so the
decision is visible rather than buried in a merge commit.

* Notes

Found 2026-08-19 while landing MR !104-!107. Filed rather than mentioned
in passing, because a red master is exactly the kind of thing that
becomes normal if nobody writes it down — and it sits directly on the
path to =release-1.9.0=, where a red pipeline is not a cosmetic problem.
